#: src/vim.md:388
msgid ""
"Zed's key bindings are evaluated only when the `\"context\"` property "
"matches your location in the editor. For example, if you add key bindings to "
"the `\"Editor\"` context, they will only work when you're editing a file. If "
"you add key bindings to the `\"Workspace\"` context, they will work "
"everywhere in Zed. Here's an example of a key binding that saves when you're "
"editing a file:"
msgstr ""

#: src/vim.md:392
msgid "\"Editor\""
msgstr ""

#: src/vim.md:394 src/vim.md:406
msgid "\"ctrl-s\""
msgstr ""

#: src/vim.md:394 src/vim.md:406 src/vim.md:567
msgid "\"workspace::Save\""
msgstr ""

#: src/vim.md:399
msgid ""
"Contexts are nested, so when you're editing a file, the context is the "
"`\"Editor\"` context, which is inside the `\"Pane\"` context, which is "
"inside the `\"Workspace\"` context. That's why any key bindings you add to "
"the `\"Workspace\"` context will work when you're editing a file. Here's an "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/vim.md:402
msgid ""
"// This key binding will work when you're editing a file. It comes built "
"into Zed by default as the workspace: save command.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/vim.md:404
msgid "\"Workspace\""
msgstr ""

#: src/vim.md:411
msgid ""
"Contexts are expressions. They support boolean operators like `&&` (and) and "
"`||` (or). For example, you can use the context `\"Editor && vim_mode == "
"normal\"` to create key bindings that only work when you're editing a file "
"_and_ you're in vim's normal mode."
msgstr ""

